==========================================================================
(2) THE PINCH t=1/(a+b) IS THE SIEVE: lonely iff no runner divisible by (a+b)
==========================================================================
  so the pinch-pigeonhole 'some pinch clears all' = 'some s<=14 has no multiple'
  = NOT sieve-covered. A counterexample is SIEVE-COVERED (A1) -> defeats it.

==========================================================================
(3) VERIFY: sieve-covered sets have NO lonely rational t=p/q (q<=14), yet ARE
    lonely at a FINE (denominator > 14) time -- out of reach of the pinch.
==========================================================================
  of 40 sieve-covered primitive 13-sets: NO rational pinch lonely AND lonely-fine for 40/40.
  examples (rational-pinch-lonely?, fine-lonely?, fine t): [(False, True, 0.00893), (False, True, 0.02232), (False, True, 0.07271), (False, True, 0.01021), (False, True, 0.00376)]

==========================================================================
VERDICT
==========================================================================
  The pinch-time pigeonhole is the RIGHT SHAPE, but every rational pinch t=p/q
  (q<=n) is a denominator-sieve witness (THM-369), lonely iff no runner divisible
  by q. A counterexample is SIEVE-COVERED (A1) -- it has a multiple of every q<=n
  -- so it defeats EVERY rational pinch BY CONSTRUCTION; a single multiple of n
  spoils every n-gon vertex t=j/n (that runner sits at 0). The pinch pigeonhole
  thus = the sieve; it settles the decorrelated majority but NOT the sieve-covered
  core. The genuine lonely time of a counterexample-candidate lives at a FINE
  denominator > n (S18 fine regime) -- where the pinch/sieve cannot reach. To
  beat the core one needs irrational/fine pinches (the measure core S550, or the
  windows S552), which carry the unresolved multiples-of-n* coupling.
